30,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Ryuji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Yumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The minutes show that the covered spots have been held by the same flats for years without review. Also, the complaint log reveals several disputes over bikes leaning on each other that never got resolved.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"I noticed that some people have to move other bikes just to get their own out in the morning! Also, the daily riders seem okay with paying a fee if it means a fairer system.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"I've confirmed that the morning rush is problematic. I saw several instances where residents had to shift other bikes to leave, and the rain exposure varies significantly across the rows.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"It's typical that our ledger is practically empty. We've spent nothing on improvements, and the current cash collection is already a tedious chore for the treasurer. Adding complex per-spot billing would just be more paperwork.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The neighboring block tried a monthly charge, but it caused friction and led to some residents avoiding meetings entirely. It seems financial penalties can damage the community atmosphere.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Toshio, that's a concerning point. If we implement a system that creates friction or resentment, it could permanently damage the atmosphere in our lobby. We should avoid anything that makes neighbors avoid each other.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Ryuji, it's so sad that people have to struggle with other bikes just to leave! We should definitely find a way to make the morning rush smoother for everyone.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"It is a matter of efficiency and respect. If the layout forces people to shove bikes aside, it's only a matter of time before someone's property gets damaged or a real argument breaks out.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Yumi, while your empathy is touching, ""fairness"" usually comes with a price tag. If we want a smoother rush, we need a system that doesn't turn our treasurer into a full-time accountant.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,I agree with Shunichi. The handover file indicates that reconciling current collections already takes three hours a month. Adding a complex billing system would be a significant administrative burden that might not be sustainable.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"If we prioritize administrative ease over a fair review, we risk letting the current imbalances fester. However, we must ensure the solution doesn't create a climate of hostility or avoidance among residents.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Yumi,,I agree! We want everyone to be happy and smiling. Maybe there's a way to reorganize the spots so the daily riders are easier to access without needing a complicated payment system?,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"Reorganizing the spots is a start, but we must address the rain exposure. If we only prioritize access, some residents will be stuck with wet saddles every morning, which is an unfair trade-off.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,Let's be realistic. Reorganizing spots is just moving the problem around. Unless we're planning to build a roof over the whole lot—which our empty ledger suggests we can't afford—someone is always going to be unhappy.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"We must consider who will actually maintain the system. If we create a complex rotation or a detailed billing plan, it will likely lapse once the initial enthusiasm fades, leaving us back where we started.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"We need a solution that is sustainable and preserves the peace. If we avoid complex billing and rotation, we can prevent the administrative burnout Toshio mentioned and the social friction I'm worried about.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"I just want everyone to get along! If we can find a simple way to make sure the daily riders aren't blocked and the spots are shared fairly, I think everyone will be much happier.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,The core issue is the physical conflict during the morning rush and the disparity in weather protection. Any solution that ignores these practical realities will only lead to more complaints and damaged bicycles.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"It's charming how we're circling the same drain. We've established that we have no money, no patience for paperwork, and a fragile social climate. Let's stop dreaming and look for the path of least resistance.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"The path of least resistance is usually the most durable. If we avoid high-maintenance rules and financial tracking, we ensure the system doesn't collapse the moment the current organizers get tired. Simple is sustainable.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Ryuji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Yumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is out; as Toshio noted, financial charges can create friction and administrative burdens. Option B is too rigid and could lead to resentment if some are consistently unlucky. Option C seems the most logical.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Option C sounds wonderful! If we use shared racks, no one feels left out or stuck with a ""bad"" spot forever. It's a great way to make sure everyone is treated equally and stays friendly!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C addresses the fairness of the spots, but double-tier racks can be cumbersome. However, it eliminates the ""ownership"" that leads to the current shoving matches and disputes over covered areas.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option C is the only one that doesn't turn our treasurer into a martyr. No billing, no lottery lists to maintain—just a simple ""first come, first served"" that lets us all stop talking about this.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option C is the most sustainable. It removes the need for monthly reconciliation and the risk of arrears, ensuring the system doesn't collapse due to administrative burnout. I vote for C.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Yumi, I agree that equality helps maintain a friendly atmosphere. By removing fixed ownership, we eliminate the source of the disputes I saw in the complaint logs and prevent the social friction Toshio warned about.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Asuka, exactly! It's so much better when everyone just shares. Plus, if we have open racks, the daily riders can just pick the most convenient spot each time, which should stop the morning shoving matches!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Yumi, while it stops fixed ownership, ""first come, first served"" might actually intensify the morning rush if everyone competes for the best spots. However, it is still more rational than the other options.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Ryuji, a little morning competition is a small price to pay for not having to manage a spreadsheet of payments. It's a far more elegant solution than the alternatives.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, a slight increase in morning competition is preferable to a system that requires constant oversight. Option C requires zero monthly paperwork, making it the only choice that won't eventually be abandoned.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, while competition for spots is possible, it is a far more natural interaction than the lingering resentment caused by fixed, unfair allocations. It keeps the conflict temporary rather than structural.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"I agree! A little bit of morning energy is better than people feeling sad or ignored. If we all just help each other out with the racks, it'll be a great way to start the day!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"I must disagree with the idea that ""morning energy"" is a positive. Shoving matches are not energy; they are a risk to property and peace. However, Option C is the only one that removes the structural unfairness of the covered spots.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Ryuji, your commitment to the tragedy of a scratched fender is impressive, but let's be honest: we can't afford a roof and we can't afford a bookkeeper. Option C is the only rational exit.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Shunichi, exactly. We cannot afford to build a system that relies on a level of diligence we've already proven we don't have. Option C is the only one that doesn't create a new job for someone.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,Option C is the only choice that balances administrative simplicity with social stability. It prevents the long-term resentment of fixed spots and the friction of payments. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I'm so happy we all agree! Option C is the kindest way to make sure everyone feels welcome and equal. I'm voting for Option C!,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"Despite my concerns about the morning rush, Option C is the most reasonable choice. It eliminates the structural inequality of the covered spots and avoids the administrative failures of the other options. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,I'll vote for Option C. It's the only choice that doesn't require a miracle of administrative willpower or a sudden influx of cash. Let's just get this over with.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I have already cast my vote for Option C. It is the only solution that ensures the system remains functional without relying on a volunteer's endless patience.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over.""}]}"
80,3,4,pass,Toshio,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
